---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ContentDownload from '../../components/ContentDownload.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { siteUrl } from '../../data/appKnowledge';
import { articleUrl, stargazingWeatherAppAndroid } from '../../data/stargazingWeatherAppAndroid';
import { translations } from '../../i18n/ui';

const t = translations.en;
const article = stargazingWeatherAppAndroid;
const pageUrl = articleUrl();
const formattedPublishedAt = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en', {
  year: 'numeric',
  month: 'long',
  day: 'numeric',
  timeZone: 'UTC',
}).format(new Date(`${article.publishedAt}T00:00:00Z`));

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: article.headline,
    name: article.title,
    description: article.metaDescription,
    url: pageUrl,
    inLanguage: 'en',
    datePublished: article.publishedAt,
    dateModified: article.publishedAt,
    author: {
      '@type': 'Person',
      name: article.author,
    },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'Stargazing Hub',
      url: siteUrl,
    },
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    inLanguage: 'en',
    mainEntity: article.faq.map((item) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: item.question,
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: item.answer,
      },
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 1,
        name: 'Home',
        item: siteUrl,
      },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 2,
        name: 'Astronomy weather app for Android',
        item: pageUrl,
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={article.title}
  description={article.metaDescription}
  image="/press/stargazinghub-observing-forecast.webp"
  lang="en"
  markdownUrl={article.markdownPath}
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <main class="sg-subpage seo-article">
    <section class="sg-subpage-hero">
      <div class="sg-shell">
        <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="sg-breadcrumb">
          <ol class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-sm text-[var(--text-muted)]">
            <li><a href="/" class="transition-colors hover:text-white">Stargazing Hub</a></li>
            <li aria-hidden="true">/</li>
            <li class="text-[var(--text-secondary)]" aria-current="page">Astronomy weather app for Android</li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div class="sg-index-intro night-reveal">
          <h1 class="sg-subpage-title">{article.headline}</h1>
          <p class="sg-subpage-lead"><strong>{article.pricingNote}</strong></p>
          <p class="article-ctas">
            <a href={article.heroCtas.googlePlay} data-store="google-play" data-placement="seo-android-hero">Get it on Google Play</a>
            <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>
            <a href={article.heroCtas.appStore} data-store="app-store" data-placement="seo-android-hero">Download on the App Store</a>
          </p>
          <p class="sg-reviewed">
            Published <time datetime={article.publishedAt}>{formattedPublishedAt}</time>
            · {article.author}
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sg-subpage-section sg-subpage-section-tight">
      <div class="sg-shell article-prose">
        <p>
          A normal weather app tells you whether it will rain. Stargazers and astrophotographers need to know more: whether it will be <strong>dark, clear, steady and transparent</strong> at 1 a.m., at the site you're driving to. <strong>Stargazing Hub</strong> is a stargazing forecast app for Android and iOS, built around those questions.
        </p>

        <h2>Hourly forecasts built on ECMWF and ICON data</h2>
        <p>
          Stargazing Hub's forecasts are based on <strong>ECMWF</strong> and <strong>ICON</strong> numerical weather model data, processed by Stargazing Hub's servers into hourly forecasts for stargazing.
          When both models agree on a clear night, you can plan with more confidence. When they disagree, open the hourly tables and see which hours, and which cloud layer, are in doubt.
        </p>
        <p>Need a longer view? A <strong>15-day forecast</strong> helps you shortlist nights before you check the hourly detail.</p>

        <h2>Cloud layers, not just "partly cloudy"</h2>
        <p>For each hour you get <strong>total cloud</strong> plus <strong>low, mid and high cloud</strong> separately:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Low cloud</strong> usually ruins a session outright.</li>
          <li><strong>High, thin cloud</strong> may still let bright targets through, but it hurts faint deep-sky work.</li>
          <li>A cloud profile view shows at what height the cloud sits.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Seeing and transparency</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Transparency</strong>: how clear the air column is. It matters most for faint galaxies, nebulae and the Milky Way.</li>
          <li><strong>Seeing</strong>: how steady the air is. It matters most for the Moon, planets and double stars at high magnification.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Stargazing Hub shows both in its hourly forecast.</p>

        <h2>The rest of the weather that matters at night</h2>
        <p>
          Hourly <strong>temperature, dew point, humidity, wind speed and direction, gusts and precipitation</strong>: everything you need to judge dew on your optics, wind shake on a long exposure, or a cold night out.
        </p>

        <h2>Stargazing index: compare nights at a glance</h2>
        <p>
          The <strong>stargazing index</strong> rates conditions from <em>terrible</em> to <em>excellent</em> so you can compare nights quickly. A <strong>Stargazing Index Map</strong> shows how conditions vary across the region around you. The index is a summary, so before you go, still check the hourly cloud, moonlight, wind and dew risk.
        </p>

        <h2>Darkness, Moon and light pollution in the same place</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Twilight timetable</strong>: civil, nautical and astronomical dusk and dawn, so you know when real darkness starts.</li>
          <li><strong>Moon</strong>: phase, plus rise and set times.</li>
          <li><strong>Light pollution map layer</strong>: tap any point to see estimated SQM and Bortle class, and browse recommended stargazing spots.</li>
          <li><strong>Clear-night map</strong>: long-term clear-night rates computed from about 20 years of climate data. Useful for choosing where to travel or which season to go.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Made for Android</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Home-screen widgets</strong> (6 types): moon phase, dark-sky window and meteor widgets are free; moon calendar, planets and next-event widgets come with membership. Widgets are also available on iOS.</li>
          <li><strong>{article.pricingNote}</strong> See the FAQ below.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>A simple workflow before every session</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Use the <strong>15-day forecast</strong> and the <strong>stargazing index</strong> to pick candidate nights.</li>
          <li>Open the <strong>ECMWF and ICON hourly</strong> tables, check low cloud first, then mid and high.</li>
          <li>Check <strong>seeing</strong> (planets) or <strong>transparency</strong> (deep sky) for your target.</li>
          <li>Confirm <strong>astronomical darkness</strong> and <strong>moonrise and moonset</strong>.</li>
          <li>Pick a darker site on the <strong>light pollution map</strong>, then check <strong>dew point</strong> and <strong>wind</strong> before you pack.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>More than weather</h2>
        <p>
          When the sky cooperates, the same app helps you use it: meteor shower forecasts with hourly predicted rates and radiant altitude, aurora probability (based on NOAA SWPC short-term forecast products; web map at our sister site <a href={article.auroraMapUrl}>AuroraMap.app</a>), a sky chart with camera AR alignment, and star recognition from photos.
        </p>

        <h3>Plan your next clear night</h3>
        <p class="article-ctas">
          <a href={article.bottomCtas.googlePlay} data-store="google-play" data-placement="seo-android-bottom">Get it on Google Play</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>
          <a href={article.bottomCtas.appStore} data-store="app-store" data-placement="seo-android-bottom">Download on the App Store</a>
        </p>

        <h2>FAQ</h2>
        {article.faq.map((item, index) => (
          <details id={`question-${index + 1}`} class="faq-question" open={index === 0}>
            <summary><h3>{item.question}</h3><span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              {item.answer.split('\n').map((line) => <p>{line}</p>)}
            </div>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <ContentDownload placement="seo-android-download" />
  </main>
  <Footer t={t} lang="en" />
</Layout>

<style>
  .article-prose {
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  .article-prose p,
  .article-prose li {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .article-prose p + p,
  .article-prose p + ul,
  .article-prose p + ol,
  .article-prose ul + p,
  .article-prose ol + p {
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
  }

  .article-prose h2 {
    margin: 2.6rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .article-prose h3 {
    margin: 2rem 0 0.8rem;
    font-size: 1.35rem;
  }

  .article-prose ul,
  .article-prose ol {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    margin: 0.8rem 0 1.2rem;
  }

  .article-prose li + li {
    margin-top: 0.55rem;
  }

  .article-prose a {
    color: var(--sg-accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.18em;
  }

  .article-ctas {
    margin-top: 1.4rem;
    font-size: 17px;
  }

  .article-ctas a {
    color: var(--sg-accent);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.18em;
  }

  .seo-article .faq-question summary h3 {
    font-size: 23px;
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .seo-article :global(section),
    .seo-article :global(div),
    .seo-article :global(p),
    .seo-article :global(h1),
    .seo-article :global(h2),
    .seo-article :global(h3) {
      word-break: normal;
      hyphens: none;
    }
  }
</style>
